## 1. Introduction
1. IntroductionSupercapacitors (SCs) with unique properties including high power density, fast charge–discharge ability, and long-term cycle life have attracted ever increasing attention in the fields of electric devices [1]. Unfortunately, the use of SCs often undergoes the low energy density. They are therefore an urgent task in exploring ideal materials for the swift storage and release of energy [2–5]. Currently, carbonaceous materials with large specific surface and relatively low cost are the most potential materials, boosting the transformation of the researched achievements effectively from the laboratory into the macroscopic real-world [6]. In principle, the electrochemical performance of carbonaceous materials mainly depends on the formation of electric double layer (EDL) and chemical and physical interactions at their surfaces. Therefore, the key point of preparing the superior carbonaceous materials with tailored structural and morphological properties including pore size distributions, pore shape, pore sizes. etc [7–9].
Heretofore, carbonaceous materials are mostly prepared by heating a diversity of suitable carbon-rich precursors in inert atmospheres, owing to their exceptional flexibility and simplicity [7]. However, some common carbon-rich precursors such as abundant biomass [10] and polymer-based hydrogels [11,12], generally possess the uncontrollable structure after carbonization, which cannot satisfy further optimization. Recently, the metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) with inorganic–organic hybrid porous structure have been exploited to synthesis tailored carbonaceous materials with uniform morphology and size. For example, Jiang et al. reported a coin-shaped porous carbon (CPC) through the facile carbonization and NaOH-assisted washing processes of aluminum-based MOFs [13]. And the CPC presented a large surface area of \(1508\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and suitable mesopores of \(6.35\;\mathrm{nm}\), deriving from the pyrolysis of carboxyl and the dissolution of \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) particles. This CPC-based symmetric solid SCs delivered a high energy density of 36.5 Wh \(\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\), maintaining an excellent power density of \(22\;\mathrm{kW}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\). Besides, Jayaramulu et al. reported a scalable strategy for the preparation of 2D nanoporous carbon sheets (NPSs) by a controlled transformation of rod-shaped potassium-based MOFs [6]. The controlled transformation of which was mainly depend on the two-step carbonization of MOFs and the removal of the potassium-based impurities. The obtained 2D NPSs presented a controllable porosity, resulting from a suitable choice of self-sacrificial precursor of MOFs. The symmetric SCs assembled with 2D NPSs displayed a battery-level energy density of 90 Wh \(\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at a capacitor-level power density. Based on the above analysis, the pyrolyzation of organic groups and the removal of metal can be certified as a topologically-ordered microstructure-forming process. Therefore, the MOFs have been considered as one of the most promising precursors/templates to synthesize carbonaceous materials with tunable porous architecture, which is in favor of achieving satisfactory SCs.
However, the further improvement of MOFs-derived porous carbon electrodes is still hindered by several challenges, as following [14–16]: i) The poor graphitic crystallinity of MOFs-derived porous carbon leads to low electrical conductivity; ii) The aggregation of MOFs-derived porous carbon is unavoidable, resulting from the high surface energy of nanosized particles. And iii) The "dead surface" [1,14,17] of MOFs-derived porous carbon electrodes still exists, owing to the addition of polymer binders. These issues reduce the utilization of active sites and impede the transfer of ions and electrons through the electrode materials.
In order to solve the mentioned above problems, a variety of three-dimensional conductive networks, such as NF [18] and textile [20], have been developed for loading MOFs derived carbon-based films or coatings, which can effectively shorten the
ion diffusion path, accelerate the transmission of ions and electrons, enhance the adhesion between carbon materials and the substrate, and increase the charging life [18–20]. For instance, Li et al. developed a helical copper-based hierarchical carbon material from the epitaxial Cu-MOF film on the textile via heating treatment [20]. In which, the cotton textile was transformed into carbon tube material after carbonization, providing more channels for ions-transport. And this material presented an excellent areal capacitance of \(1812\;\mathrm{mF\;cm}^{-2}\), due to the synergistic effect of MOF- and textile-derived carbon materials. Besides, the addition of pseudocapacitive materials such as Ni/Co-based materials can further improve the cycling and capacitive properties of the composites. Lee et al. prepared a 2D ultra-thin nano-accedion Ni-MOF@NiSi2@C (NMSC) from a nickel-based MOF on NF, and the NMSC presented a core–shell hetero-nanostructure, which can offer superior charge transfer ability and remarkable specific capacitance (up to \(283.5\mathrm{F\,g}^{-1}\)) [21]. Although these electrodes with 2D MOFs-derived carbon-based films or coatings are of unique structures and high specific capacitances, these low-dimensional structures would present a positive trend of aggregation, so that these materials are difficult to applied in practical applications [22–23].
Of late, with the wide application of electronic equipment, the problem of the reuse and waste disposal of the equipment is hard to ignore once its lifetimes are over [24]. Fortunately, it has been demonstrated that porous carbon materials are not only highly efficient electrodes, but also excellent adsorbents that can remove Pb (II) from wastewater [25]. In fact, even at low concentration level, toxic Pb (II) can lead to severe damage to central nervous system and reproductive system [26–28].
Considering all these in mind, here we tried to combine the concepts of SCs and adsorption technologies together to achieve a more environment-friendly and low-cost method of exploiting both an efficient energy storage device and an adsorbent. Herein, the 3D urchin-like MOFs-derived carbon materials with Co-adding were anchored on NF (Fig. 1), named as UAC@NF. Multidimensional urchin-like hierarchical architecture may help them prevent aggregation, enhance their long-term stability, and offer high specific capacitances, which is currently less reported. The areal capacitance of UAC@NF could achieve \(524.6\;\mathrm{mF\;cm}^{-2}\) at \(1\;\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\). And then, we combined the UAC@NF and high temperatures activated graphite carbon felt (GF500) to make a binder-free asymmetric SC for the first time, in contrast to most reported MOF-derived porous carbon electrodes [29–32]. The asymmetric SC displayed an energy density of \(0.036\;\mathrm{mWh\,cm}^{-3}\) at a power density of \(0.984\;\mathrm{mW\;cm}^{-3}\); also, it showed excellent stability (91.4% retention over 10,000 cycles). In addition, the used UAC@NF maintained the initial morphology, which allowed the used UAC@NF to be reutilized to recycle Pb (II) from sewage. This "waste to wealth" way would present a remarkable impact on energy and the environment.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
Graphite carbon felt (GF, thickness: \(1.5\;\mathrm{mm}\), bulk density: \(0.035\;\mathrm{g\,cm}^{-3}\)) and nickel foam (NF, thickness: \(2.0\;\mathrm{mm}\), bulk density: \(0.23\;\mathrm{g\,cm}^{-3}\)) were obtained from Kunshan Long-shengbao Electronic Material Store, China. Co \((\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and trimesic acid were purchased from Tianjin Sailboat Chemical Reagent Technology Co., Ltd. polyvinylpyrrolidone K40 (PVP-40, \(\mathrm{Mw}\approx40000\)) were bought from Sigma-Aldrich (USA). All reagents and solvents were of analytical grade and were used as received.
2.2. Synthesis of UAC@NF electrodes
0.7 mmol Co \((\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), 0.02 mmol PVP-40, 0.5 mmol trimesic acid were dissolved in \(20\,\mathrm{mL}\) mixed solution (Vethanol: V N, N- dimethylformamide \(=1\): 1). The precursor solution was stirred for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) to obtain a uniform, pink-red solution followed by transferring into a \(100\,\mathrm{mL}\) Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave. The NF (approximately \(2\,\mathrm{cm}\,\times\,2\,\mathrm{cm}\)) was then immersed into the obtained solution as the growth substrate. For in-situ MOFs growth, the autoclave was sealed and maintained at \(160\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{h}\). After the solvothermal process, the as-synthesized material was rinsed with absolute ethanol and deionized water several times and dried at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in an oven; the urchin-like MOFs could be obtained on the NF substrate, which was denoted as MOFs@NF. The MOFs@NF was heated in tube furnace at \(350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(3\,\mathrm{h}\) under Ar atmosphere to acquire urchin-like amorphous carbon on NF, which was labeled as UAC@NF. The average mass loading of MOFs and UAC were \(18.7\,\mathrm{mg\,cm}^{-3}\) and \(10.2\,\mathrm{mg\,cm}^{-3}\), respectively.
## 2.3. Synthesis of GF500 electrodes
The commercial GF (approximately \(2\,\mathrm{cm}\times2\,\mathrm{cm}\)) was heated at \(500\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(1\,\mathrm{h}\) under air atmosphere to obtain activated GF, which was denoted as GF500. The mass density of GF500 is \(\approx27.1\,\mathrm{mg\,cm}^{-3}\).
## 2.4. Electrochemical measurements
All electrochemical tests, including cyclic voltammetry (CV), galvanostatic charge–discharge (GCD), cycling stability, and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS), were measured by a CHI 760E (Chenhua Shanghai) electrochemical workstation in 3 M KOH aqueous solution. In a three-electrode system, UAC@NF and GF500 were used as working electrode, platinum plate was served as counter electrode, and saturated calomel electrode (SCE) electrode was acted as reference electrode. The area of the electrode was \(1\times1\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\). The asymmetric cell SCs were assembled with two free-standing electrodes: UAC@NF was acted as positive electrode and GF500 was used as negative electrode. A nonwoven was soaked in 3 M KOH, serving as the separator; Titanium plate was used as the current collector.
The specific capacitance \(C\,(\mathrm{F\,cm}^{-3})\), energy density \((\mathrm{Wh\,cm}^{-3})\) and power density \((\mathrm{W\,cm}^{-3})\) were calculated by the following equations [33]:
\[
\mathsf{C}=\frac{I}{V}\int\left(1/V(t)\right)dt
\]
\[
\mathsf{E}=\frac{\mathsf{C}\Delta V^2}{2\times3600}
\]
\[
\mathsf{p}=\frac{E\times3600}{t}
\]
Where V is the total volume of the electrode, \(I\) is the discharge current, \(V(t)\) is the potential as a function of \(t\), \(t\) is the discharge time, and \(\Delta V\) is the potential.
## 2.5. Materials characterization
The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was taken on a Thermo Scientific K-Alpha. The Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) was measured on Thermo Fisher (NCOLET iN10, USA). The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were obtained by a Bruke D8 Advance. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images including energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) were recorded on Zeiss Merlin Compact. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) coupled with high-resolution TEM (HRTEM)) images were taken on Zeiss Merlin Compact and Tecnai G2 F20 S-TWIN. The graphitic nature of the obtained electrode was studied using a Raman spectroscopy (Horiba Scientific LabRAM) with an excitation wavelength of \(532\,\mathrm{nm}\). The specific surface area and pore size were measured by an ASAP 2020 PLUS HDB8 Surface Area and Porosity Analyzer. Electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectroscopy was recorded on a Bruker EMX plus EPR spectrometer at room temperature. The concentration of metal ions was measured by Inductively Coupled Plasma Mass Spectrometry (ICP-MS, Agilent 7900).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization
The schematic of the preparation process of UAC@NF was presented in Fig. 1. Typically, the urchin-like MOFs were anchored on NF through the simple coordination of \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) with the trimesic acid ligand. The PVP surfactant was not only acted as C source but employed to tune the morphology and size of MOFs [34]. Then, the CoO in the MOFs@NF was reduced to metallic Co and encased in amorphous carbon during the carbonization process. Finally, the as-expected urchin-like amorphous carbon (UAC@NF) was obtained with the intrinsic morphologies of MOFs@NF. The digital photographs were presented in Fig. S1.
SEM images of the MOFs@NF and UAC@NF were shown in Fig. 2. The SEM images of the MOFs@NF showed plenty of comparatively uniform and unbroken spherical MOFs with a diameter of \(\approx2\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) stacked on the entire surface of NF, and the surface of the microspheres was covered with a layer of evenly distributed nanoneedles with \(600{-}800\,\mathrm{nm}\) diameter. After the annealing treatment, some of the precursors on the surface of NF were partially shedding. And the obtained UAC@NF maintained the initial urchin-like structure of precursor, wherein scattering superstructures could reduce the contact area between urchin-like microspheres and the likelihood of aggregation [23]. In addition, according to the EDS results in Table S1, the atomic % of C element increased from 42.92 to 62.89 after carbonization. However, the O element significantly decreased. The elements Co and Ni remained the same
as in the UAC@NF. The element mapping (Fig. 2g) showed a relatively uniform distribution of each element in the UAC@NF.
The structures of as-prepared samples were characterized by TEM measurement. From Fig. 3a-b, it was obviously that the urchin-like MOFs and UAC microspheres were both composed of extensive thin nanosheets. However, the HRTEM images (Fig. 3cd) revealed that the crystal structure of MOFs and UAC were different. As denoted in the Fig. 3c, MOFs consisted of only one fringe spacing of \(0.25\;\mathrm{nm}\), matching with the (111) plane of CoO [35]. As for the HRTEM image of UAC in Fig. 3d, the lattice fringes with spacing of \(0.25\;\mathrm{nm}\) and \(0.20\;\mathrm{nm}\) were assigned to CoO (111) plane and Co (111) plane, respectively [36]. And the Co cores were surrounded by amorphous carbon (read cycle in Fig. 3d). These results confirmed that some Co were uniformly anchored on the special porous carbon after carbonization, forming a unique \(\mathsf{C o}/\mathsf{C}\) heterostructure, which can provide more surface-active centers and improve the total electronic conductivity of UAC@NF [37–39].
The composition and structure characteristic of MOFs@NF and UAC@NF were further analyzed in detail. The FTIR patterns of the as-prepared materials were shown in Fig. 4a. For MOFs@NF, the broad peak at \(3427\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) was attributed to the stretching vibration of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) molecules existing in MOFs@NF structure [40,41]. The peaks at 1618 and \(1567\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) were ascribed to the asymmetric and symmetric stretching vibrations of –COO group, respectively. And this separation of these two stretching vibrations demonstrated that there was coordination between –COO group and Co ion [42]. These evidences confirmed that the synthesized MOFs@NF was a kind of MOFs structure [43]. For UAC@NF, the peak at \(3303\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) was attributed to the stretching vibration of \(\mathrm{OH}^{-}\) [44]. The peak at \(1638\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) was assigned with the stretching vibration of \(\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{C}\) in the aromatic rings. The peak at \(551\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) was ascribed to the stretching vibrations of the Co-O bonds [36]. In Fig. S2, raw NF showed a zeta potential (ζ-Pot) value of \(4.69\pm0.2\:\mathrm{mV}\), whereas MOFs@NF displayed a ζ-Pot value of \(-8.1\pm0.1\:\mathrm{mV}\). The reduction of charge represented that there were many –COOH groups converging on the surface of MOFs@NF [45],
which was consistent with FTIR results. Furthermore, UAC@NF presented a lower \(\zeta\)-Pot value of \(-7.8\pm0.1\:\mathrm{mV}\) than MOFs@NF, indicative of the removal of –COOH group, in good agreement with the FTIR.
The surface chemical properties of MOFs@NF and UAC@NF were investigated by XPS, which were shown in Fig. 4e, f and S3. In Fig. S3a, the strong in-signals indicated the presence of Co, Ni, C, and O in MOFs@NF and UAC@NF. Based on Fig. S3b, the atomic % of C element increased from 24.22 to 48.44 after carbonization
and the atomic \(\%\) of Co element slightly decreased from 5.55 to 4.48. As shown in Fig. 4e, the high-resolution Co 2p spectrum of MOFs@NF revealed the presence of Co \(2{\tt p}_{3/2}\) at \(780.6\,{\mathrm{eV}}\) and Co \(2{\tt p}_{1/2}\) at \(796.5\,{\mathrm{eV}}\). Additionally, the peaks at \(785.7\,{\mathrm{eV}}\) and \(802.2\,{\mathrm{eV}}\) were the evidence of Co (II) oxide in MOFs@NF [37,38,49]. Compared with the MOFs@NF, there was a small peak at \(774.2\,{\mathrm{eV}}\) in the high-resolution Co 2p spectrum of UAC@NF, which could be assigned to metallic Co [50,51], corresponding to the TEM results.
Density functional theory (DFT) calculations were therefore implemented to demonstrate the structure of UAC@NF. The simplified model of UAC@NF before and after Co-adding was shown in Fig. S5. Total density of states (DOS) of UAC@NF showed an additional peak above the Fermi level after the addition of Co (Fig. S6), demonstrating that the introduction of Co significantly enhanced the chemical reactivity of UAC@NF.
## 3.2. Electrochemical properties
According to the outstanding microstructural data of UAC@NF, we further explored the electrochemical performances of this hybrid. The electrochemical tests of raw NF, MOFs@NF, and UAC@NF were conducted in 3 M KOH using a standard three-electrode system. Fig. 5a showed the CV curves of raw NF, MOFs@NF, and UAC@NF electrodes in the potential window \(0-\) \(0.7\,\mathrm{V}\) at \(50\,\mathrm{mV}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\). From the CV curves, it was clearly that the integral area of UAC@NF was much larger than those of raw NF and MOFs@NF. This observation suggested that the UAC@NF has the
largest capacitance value. Fig. 5b displayed the GCD curves of raw NF, MOFs@NF, and UAC@NF electrodes in the potential window \(0{-}0.7\,\mathrm{V}\) at \(5\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\). As conducted from the GCD curves, the specific capacitance values of raw NF, MOFs@NF, and UAC@NF were \(20\,\mathrm{mF\,cm}^{-2}\), \(28.6\,\mathrm{mF\,cm}^{-2}\), and \(385.71\,\mathrm{mF\,cm}^{-2}\), respectively. EIS was performed to obtain deep insight into the superior electrochemical performance of UAC@NF. As shown in Fig. 5c and S4a, the resistance radius of UAC@NF was lowest, implying that UAC@NF presented the highest ability of electron transfer [52]. Fig. 5d displayed the CV curves of UAC@NF at \(2{-}100\,\mathrm{mV}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) and the CV curves remained a similar quasi-rectangular shape, suggesting that the EDLC was the major contribution to overall energy storage [53]. In addition, the Co-adding with reversible faradic reactions could improve the specific capacitance. As shown in Fig. 5a and Fig. S4b, the percentages of the pseudo-capacitive contribution by the Co-adding to the total capacity were \(12.25\%\sim\) \(48.67\%\) at \(2{-}100\,\mathrm{mV}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\). Furthermore, a series of GCD profiles at difference current densities \((1{-}10\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1})\) were shown in Fig. 5e. According to the GCD profiles, the UAC@NF provided \(524.6\,\mathrm{mF}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) at \(1\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) (Fig. 5f), which outperformed those of previous reports for MOFs-derived carbon-based electrode at the same current density [30,54–56]. And it was obvious that the rate capability performance showed a further decrease at high current densities, resulting from the finite diffusion time imposes restrictions on the movement of ions at high current densities [57].
Based on the experiments and theoretical calculations, the relationship of UAC@NF between their structure and excellent electrochemical properties was discussed. First, this integrated
configuration including the direct growth of UAC on 3D NF with high conductivity not only accelerated the electronic and electrolyte transmission but prevented the raise in electrode resistance resulting from the polymer binders [57]. Next, the highly vertically oriented structure of urchin-like UAC guaranteed effective and rapid transport of electrons and ions [52]. Finally, the \(\mathrm{Co/C}\) heterostructure was formed by embedding metallic Co into the amorphous carbon, which improved the electrical conductivity of the UAC@NF [46].
Besides, both the structure and electrochemical properties of the GF500 was also investigated. The morphology and structure
of GF500 were investigated by the SEM, Raman, XRD, ESR and BET techniques (Fig. S7, 8 and Table S2). After high temperature activation, the GF500 possessed more oxygen vacancies, crystal defects and specific surface area, which could significantly improve the charge storage capacity of the material based on our previous report [58]. Therefore, the electrochemical performance of GF500 was also measured by a three-electrode device. As shown in Fig. 6a, the GF500 presented a similar quasi-rectangular shape at \(2{-}100\,\mathrm{mV}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\), implying an EDL mechanism [53]. In addition, nearly symmetric GCD curves of GF500 at \(0.6{-}10\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) were presented in Fig. 6b, indicating a highly reversible behavior. As shown
in Fig. 6c, the calculated specific capacitance was \(262.0\,\mathrm{mF\,cm}^{-2}\) at \(0.6\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\).
3.3. Electrochemical performance of the asymmetric supercapacitor device
To further confirm the value of these electrode materials in practical applications, an ASC device was assembled with UAC@NF as positive electrode and GF500 as negative electrode in \(3\,\mathrm{M}\) KOH electrolyte, which was named as UAC@NF//GF500. To obtain the optimal electrochemical performance of the ASC, the area ratio of UAC@NF to GF500 was set to \(\approx0.4\), which was calculated by the charge balance theory. As the following equation [59]:
\[
\frac{s_+}{s_-}=Cs_- \Delta V_- / Cs_+ \Delta V_+
\]
Where s \((\mathsf{c m}^{2})\) is the area of electrode, Cs, and Cs. \((\mathrm{F\,cm}^{-2})\) are the specific capacitance of the positive \((+)\) and negative \((-)\) electrodes, respectively. \(\Delta\mathbf{V}\) (V) is the potential range.
As a control, the symmetric supercapacitor devices (GF500//GF500 and UAC@NF//UAC@NF) were also assembled. Fig. 7a showed that the CV curves of ACS had no obvious deformation at different scan rate from 4 to \(100\,\mathrm{mV\,s}^{-1}\), demonstrating a stably working potential of \(0{-}1\,\mathrm{V}\) [60]. And in the wide voltage window, the shape of the CV curves was all irregular due to the synergetic effect of the two electrodes. The GCD curves of the ASC device at difference current densities from \(0.3\,-\,5\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\) were shown in Fig. 7b. Compared with the underperforming symmetric SCs, the ASC device presented a maximum specific capacitance of \(263.6\,\mathrm{mF\,cm}^{-3}\) at \(0.3\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\) (Fig. S9). The Ragone plots of the ASC and the symmetric supercapacitor devices in respect of the energy density and power density were shown in Fig. 7c, the ASC device exhibited a highest energy density of \(0.036\,\mathrm{mWh\,cm}^{-3}\) at a power density of \(0.984\,\mathrm{mW\,cm}^{-3}\), and maintained a favorable energy density of \(0.024\,\mathrm{mWh\,cm}^{-3}\) at a high-power density of \(16.64\,\mathrm{mW\,cm}^{-3}\). And the constant brightness of the LED lamps in Fig. 7d (inset), confirmed the effectiveness of the ASC in the real-life applications. Moreover, the cycle performance was crucial
for the practical application of devices. In Fig. 7d, the ACS device maintained a high-rate capability of \(91.4\%\) after 10,000 cycles, which was higher than the presently carbon-based materials in Table 1. In addition, SEM image of the UAC@NF after 10,000 cycles was present in Fig. S10. The UAC@NF still maintained its original
urchin-like structure after cycles, indicating the ultra-long life and great practical value. And there was no obvious change in the EIS spectra of the ASC device after 1st and 10000th cycles (Fig. 7e and Table S3), which was composed of a quasi-semicircle in the high-frequency region and a straight line in the high
frequency region. According to the equivalent circuit, the equivalent serial resistance value of the ASC device was estimated to be \(1.5\,\Omega\), revealing the combination of rapid electron transmission.
## 3.4. Heavy metal ions adsorption measurement
UAC@NF and GF500 were the outstanding electrodes, and the ASC composed of them showed good cycling performance for energy storage. However, the life of the equipment is usually limited. In most cases, the used devices would be directly discarded, which may cause pollution to the environment. Subsequent research found that the morphology of the materials did not change greatly after cycling (Fig. S10), which meant that the recycled materials could be used as a potential adsorbent.
The adsorption behavior of samples to Pb (II) was investigated to confirm the above conclusion. The adsorption studies were repeated for three times in room temperature, stirring \(5\;\mathrm{mg}\) of samples with \(50\;\mathrm{mL}\;20\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) Pb (II) solution, and the adsorption amount of Pb (II) was evaluated based on our previous reports [61]. As shown in Fig. 7f, UAC@NF after 10,000 cycles \((\mathrm{UAC@NF}_{10000})\) exhibited the highest removal rates for Pb (II). Typically, the removal rate of Pb (II) reached \(73.1\%\), the corresponding adsorption capacity was \(146.2\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). However, the removal rate of UAC@NF for Pb (II) was only \(15.6\%\), the corresponding adsorption capacity was \(31.2\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), which was much lower than \(\mathrm{UAC@NF}_{10000}\). Furthermore, the \(\mathrm{GF500}_{1000}\) displayed higher adsorption capacity than unused GF500. This phenomenon might be due to the increase of more micropores in the process of multiple charging-discharging, thus increasing the storage capacity of ions, but this result was still
lack of strong evidence. Those results indicated that the waste electrodes could adsorb heavy metal ions with high efficiency.
In addition, the effect of the initial ion concentration \(C_{0}\) on the equilibrium adsorption capacity \(q_{\mathrm{e}}\) and removal rate were shown in Fig. 8a. Owing to the increased driving force obtained from the concentration gradient, the adsorption capacity of \(\mathrm{UAC@NF}_{10000}\) for Pb (II) showed a linear growth in initial, and last tended to balance. The maximum adsorption capacity for Pb (II) was \(332.03\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), which was higher than most adsorbents, as shown in Table 2. The removal rates for Pb (II) continued to decline, resulting from the limited adsorption sites. Nevertheless, the removal rate of Pb (II) was always \(>\,66\%\). To further study the adsorption mechanism, the fitting of experimental data to Langmuir and Freundlich models [61] were presented in Fig. 8b and Table 3. Apparently, the adsorption of Pb (II) was better described by the Langmuir model \((\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.9999)\) than Freundlich model \((\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.9937)\). These results demonstrated that the adsorption
processes for Pb (II) by UAC@NF10000 was mainly monolayer adsorption and relatively homogeneous.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, novel hierarchical urchin-like amorphous carbon with Co-adding was fabricated via one-step carbonization of MOFs, which in-situ growing on nickel foam as an free-standing electrode (UAC@NF) for supercapacitors. The as-prepared hierarchical UAC@NF displayed a respectable specific capacitance \(524.6\,\mathrm{mF}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) at \(1\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\). And the asymmetric supercapacitor device was assembled with UAC@NF and high temperature activated graphite carbon felt (GF500), possessing high specific capacity of \(263.6\,\mathrm{mF}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\), together with a superior areal energy density of \(0.0126\,\mathrm{mWh}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\). In addition, the used UAC@NF (UAC@NF10000) could be used as a potential adsorbent and it was shown to display a high adsorption capacity of \(332.03\,\mathrm{mg}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for Pb (II). These excellent supercapacitor and adsorption performance were attributed to the 3D hierarchical urchin-like structure. Therefore, this work provided a promising approach to serve both energy crisis and the heavy metal pollution by the hierarchical urchin-like amorphous carbon.
